\f1\fs22 "Stop sweating," Chanta said. "You look like a bald sprinkler system having an anxiety attack."\
"Stop sweating? Stop sweating?!" Johnny's voice climbed to frequencies only dogs and stressed physicists could hear. "Do you know what happens if these discs resonate? We could create a temporal paradox where we arrive before we left and have to explain to ourselves why we're idiots!"\
The security guard waved them through, apparently used to Johnny looking like he was about to explode.\
At the designated drop-off point, Major Memphis and Suspicious Mick waited with a van that had "DEFINITELY NOT STOLEN REALITY-WARPING TECHNOLOGY" painted on the side in sequins.\
"Brothers!" Major Memphis beamed. "The Dawkins provides such bounty!"\
Johnny practically threw himself out of the car. "Take them! Take all of them! I don't want them anywhere near\'97" He caught himself, tried to salvage some dignity. "I mean, I may need them later. For science. Please store them somewhere cool and dry. And lead-lined. Possibly in another dimension."\
"Cool and dry?" Mick scratched his sideburn. "Like a wine cellar?"\
"NO! No wine cellars! Just... somewhere safe. Away from me. Very away."\
The Elvi shrugged, loaded the guitar cases with the casual disregard of people who didn't know they were handling doomsday devices. "The Great Teacher's friend is very specific about storage," Memphis noted. "Must be evolution. The anxious survive longer."\
After they left, Johnny stood hyperventilating while Chanta grabbed the three remaining discs. "You okay?"\
"Okay? I just transported enough Gyges Discs to make Rotherhithe look stable. My insurance doesn't cover acts of temporal instability!" He fumbled for his access card. "Come on. Before I realise what I'm doing and have a stroke."\
They made their way through Sublime Point's manicured grounds, Johnny muttering about liability and Chanta trying not to think about what he was about to do. Sarah's townhouse sat nestled between identical units, distinguishable only by the subtle security that screamed "important person lives here."\
"She's going to kill me," Chanta said.\
"She's going to kill me first," Johnny corrected. "I'm supposed to be managing her project, not smuggling her ex-whatever into\'97oh god, are you two exes? Is this a romantic thing? Am I facilitating a booty call?"\
"It's not like that."\
"It's exactly like that! I'm a cosmic Uber driver for your unresolved feelings!" Johnny stopped at Sarah's door. "I'm leaving. If security asks, you held me at gunpoint. With a disco ball. Make it believable."\
He fled before Chanta could respond, leaving him alone with a door that might as well have been a portal to another dimension.\
Chanta knocked.\
Silence.\
He knocked again.\
"Go away, Johnny! I told you I'm not discussing pentagonal resonance until\'97" The door opened. Sarah stood there, one hand on her enormous belly, the other frozen on the doorknob. Her expression cycled through surprise, anger, confusion, and something that might have been longing before settling on defensive hostility.\
"Tony? What the hell are you doing here?"\
"Hi, Sarah."\
"Hi? HI?!" She looked past him as if expecting an invasion. "How did you get in here? This place has security that makes PAPER look friendly!"\
"Johnny owed me a favour."\
"Johnny\'97of course." She didn't move from the doorway. "You can't be here."\
"I need to talk to you."\
"Well, I don't need to talk to you." But she didn't close the door. They stood there, the weight of their last encounter hanging between them like a badly written prophecy.\
"The Elvi are in trouble," Chanta said finally.\
Sarah's expression softened fractionally. "The gospel virus. I heard."\
"It's worse than you know. They're split down the middle. Brother against brother. Sequin against rhinestone." He tried for levity, failed. "the High Elvis wants to hold a concert. Rock Against Revival. At The Domain."\
"The Domain?" Sarah's eyebrows rose. "That's Airborne territory. They'd never\'97"\
"They would if Eloise Shakti asked nicely. With lawyers."\
Sarah's hand moved protectively to her stomach. "And you want me to convince Eloise to help the Elvi throw a concert?"\
"It's more than a concert. It's... hope. The High Elvis thinks reason can defeat faith. That The Dawkins\'97Richard Dawkins\'97shows the path forward. They call him The One." Chanta gestured helplessly. "And your baby..."\
"Little Clinton."\
"They call him The One After The One. They think he'll bring about a new age of scientific enlightenment. No more superstition, no more division." Chanta's voice cracked. "They need this, Sarah. And I... I need to help them."\
"Why?"\
"Because they kidnapped me once already when I couldn't write. Next time might involve less polite kidnapping and more disappointed sequins." He met her eyes. "And because I can't write without..."\
"Without what?"\
"Without being near Little Clinton. Near you." The words hung in the air like disco balls in a cathedral. "Whatever happened between us\'97however I screwed up, the music only comes when I'm close to the baby. To you."\
Sarah's knuckles were white on the doorframe. "You didn't screw up, Chanta. You just... became something else. Someone else."\
"I'm still me."\
"Are you? The Tony I knew didn't have mystical regeneration powers. Didn't make women... glow... after..." She trailed off, colour rising in her cheeks.\
"I don't understand it either. But I'm still the guy with the fairy boxers who cleaned your office. Who fell through your ceiling. Who\'97"\
"Who I can't trust." Sarah's voice was quiet. "Not after what you became in that penthouse."\
They stood in silence, two people separated by a doorway and roughly eighteen percent of the world's wealth.\
"Just a few days," Chanta said finally. "A couple of days. Let me help the Elvi. Let me write. Then I'll go."\
"And if I say no?"\
"Then I'll go now. But the Elvi will suffer. The concert will fail. And Little Clinton won't have the songs he's supposed to inspire." Chanta played his last card. "The prophecy\'97"\
"Screw the prophecy!" Sarah's outburst surprised them both. "I'm tired of prophecies and Sages and mystical pregnancies! I just want..."\
"What?"\
"Normal. I want normal."\
Chanta almost laughed. "Sarah, you're carrying a clone baby of Richard Dawkins implanted by mystical Sages to bring about universal evolution. That ship hasn't just sailed, it's achieved faster-than-light travel."\
Despite herself, Sarah's lips twitched. "When you put it like that..."\
\'93CHANTA!\'94 Kathy's voice exploded from inside the townhouse. A moment later she appeared, shoving past Sarah to envelop Chanta in a hug that threatened his ribs. "You absolute bastard! Where have you been?"\
"Getting kidnapped, becoming a billionaire, the usual." Chanta gasped for air. "Good to see you too, Kathy."\
"Sarah's been miserable without\'97OW!" Kathy rubbed her side where Sarah had elbowed her. "What? He should know!"\
"I have not been miserable."\
"She's reorganised her closet seventeen times. By colour, size, and existential weight." Kathy dragged Chanta inside. "Hayley! Guess who's here!"\
Hayley appeared at the top of the stairs, took one look at Chanta, and sighed. "Great. The cause of all our problems is back."\
"I missed you too," Chanta said.\
"Don't push it." But Hayley's stern expression cracked slightly. "You look good for someone who's been tortured on live television."\
"Clean living and fairy magic," Chanta replied.\
Sarah closed the door, decision made by committee. "Fine. A few days. You sleep on the couch. You don't go near my room. You definitely don't do... whatever it is you do to women now."\
"I don't do anything! It just happens!"\
"Well make it not happen." Sarah waddled toward the kitchen. "Kathy, no giving him ideas."\
"Would I do that?" Kathy's grin suggested she absolutely would.\
Chanta stood in the living room, suddenly unsure what to do with his hands, his feet, his entire existence. He was back in Sarah's space, close enough to feel the pull of whatever connected them, far enough to know the distance might be permanent.\
From the kitchen, Sarah called: "And Tony? If you write even one song comparing me to a pink fairy, I'll have Hayley shoot you."\
"I'd do it too," Hayley confirmed. "I've been practicing."\
Chanta smiled despite everything. He was home. Sort of. If home was a place where everyone barely tolerated you and the woman you loved was carrying a prophetic baby that wasn't yours but somehow was your muse.\
Evolution, he decided, was complicated.
